\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The quartic decreases at the marginal ratio}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{In \ref{fdip-01DG}, assume #{p\lambda^2=1} and #{c_0=1}.
The quartic coefficient of #{F^{[2]}} is
##{g'_{[2]}=g-\beta_p g^2,\qquad
\beta_p=36(p-1)/p>0.}
Fix #{T_0>0} and a compact interval #{J\subset\RR}.
For #{\nu=tg}, #{|t|\le T_0}, and #{0\le g\le\epsilon},
there is a constant #{C_J}, independent of #{g,t}, such that
##{\sup_{y\in J}|F_V(y)-F^{[2]}(y)|\le C_Jg^3.}
Here the fine Gaussian reference and #{\epsilon>0} are fixed.}

\proof{
\p{The coefficient follows from #{r^4=p} and #{M_2=p-1}.
Write #{V=g(W_4+tW_2)}. For #{|t|\le T_0}, these monic
quartics have a common lower bound and a common polynomial
growth bound. Their sums on a block therefore have uniform lower
bounds and Gaussian moment envelopes. The proof of
\ref{fdip-00GN}, through the third derivative and with #{y\in J},
gives a uniform right-hand Taylor remainder of order #{g^3}.
Its first two terms are precisely those of \ref{fdip-01DG}.}
}

\p{This estimate bounds a function on a compact field interval.
It does not by itself bound a coefficient extracted from the exact
potential on the entire real line.}
